<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Connect with Nature! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Can Connect with Nature!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Relax and Listen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe routines and simple procedures. Interactive: Collaborate verbally in team activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe outdoor routines (e.g., "I go outside in the afternoon."). Can collaborate with classmates in verbal games or team activities (e.g., "Make two teams. Together, write or draw outdoor activities on the board."). Can describe personal nature experiences to describe abilities or observations in nature (e.g., "I can see trees, but I can't see flowers.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large pictures of different nature scenes (e.g., a forest, a river, a park). The teacher asks, "What can you see?" and encourages simple responses. Then, the teacher plays a short audio clip of nature sounds (birds chirping, water flowing, wind rustling leaves) and asks, "What can you hear?" Students raise their hands and say what they hear. (e.g., "I hear birds!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using "can/can't" and present simple with nature vocabulary. For example, pointing to a picture of a park, the teacher says: "I can see a tree. The tree is green. I can hear birds. I can relax here." Then, pointing to a picture with no flowers, "I can't see flowers here." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /k/ in "can" and /s/ in "sun" by exaggerating the sounds and having students repeat chorally and individually. The teacher also models blending for "sun" /s/ /ʌ/ /n/ and "flower" /f/ /l/ /ɑʊ/ /ɚ/ using hand gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of "can" and nature vocabulary.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a bird picture: "Can I fly like a bird?" (Expected: "No, you can't.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a tree: "Is this a flower or a tree?" (Expected: "It's a tree.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to grass: "Is the grass blue?" (Expected: "No, it's green.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue between two children (Leo and Mia) exploring a park. The dialogue uses previously introduced nature vocabulary (tree, grass, flower, bird, sun, park, stream) and targets grammar (can/can't, prepositions of place, present simple) and pragmatic functions (sensory observations, locating). Example:
                    <br>Leo: "Look, Mia! We are at the park."
                    <br>Mia: "Wow! I can see a big tree. And I can hear birds singing."
                    <br>Leo: "Yes! The sun is bright. I like sitting under the tree. It's peaceful."
                    <br>Mia: "Me too! I can see a red flower near the stream."
                    <br>Leo: "Can you smell the flowers? They smell nice!"
                    <br>Mia: "Yes, I can! This is my favorite spot."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing connection with nature: Talking about sensory observations and experiences in nature (e.g., "I hear the birds." "The flowers smell nice."). Locating people and things: Identifying locations in nature (e.g., "We are at the park entrance.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (oral):</b> "Who is in the park?" "What can Mia see?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (oral):</b> "Leo can see a blue flower." (False) "The park is peaceful." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match pictures of nature items (bird, tree, flower) with phrases from the dialogue (e.g., "birds singing," "big tree," "red flower"). Students say the matching phrase.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using "Can/Can't," prepositions of place (at, near, under), Present simple, and vocabulary: tree, grass, flower, bird, leaf, park, nature, sun, sky, stream, walk, listen, look, relax, hear, see, quiet, peaceful, beautiful, green, bright.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - "I Can See" Chain:</b> The teacher starts by saying, "In nature, I can see a tree." The first student repeats and adds another nature item, e.g., "In nature, I can see a tree and a flower." The next student repeats the whole chain and adds one more, focusing on correct pronunciation of /k/ and /s/. If a student makes a mistake, the teacher gently corrects and models.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - "Can/Can't" Sentence Frame Pairs:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards (some with nature items, some without, or some depicting actions like "fly" vs. "swim"). Partner A picks a card and completes a sentence frame verbally: "I can see a ____, but I can't see a ____." or "I can ____, but I can't ____." (e.g., "I can see a butterfly, but I can't see a spider in this picture." or "I can walk, but I can't fly."). Partner B listens and confirms or corrects. Students then switch roles.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Describe My Nature Spot":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student secretly chooses one of the previously presented nature pictures (or draws a simple one in 30 seconds). Partner A describes their picture to Partner B using "I can see...", "It is...", and prepositions of place (e.g., "I can see a big tree. A bird is in the tree. The grass is green."). Partner B listens and tries to guess which picture Partner A is describing. Then they switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and noting language use.</li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Can Connect with Nature!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs: "Our Peaceful Nature Spot."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into pairs. Each pair imagines they are visiting a peaceful nature spot (e.g., a park, a river bank).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use the vocabulary and grammar learned to have a short conversation (3-4 exchanges each) about what they can see, hear, and feel. They should also express appreciation for nature and use polite requests.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example prompts/phrases for students: "I can see...", "I can hear...", "It's (quiet/peaceful/beautiful).", "I like...", "Can you pass me the (imaginary) binoculars, please?", "Would you like to sit here?", "This is my favorite spot."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students perform their short role-play for another pair or for the class if time permits. The teacher encourages natural interaction and communication over perfect accuracy at this stage.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Our Peaceful Nature Spot" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can describe at least two outdoor observations using "I can see..." or "I can hear...".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use at least three target nature vocabulary words (e.g., tree, flower, bird, park, grass, peaceful).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /k/ and /s/ is generally intelligible.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates verbally with partner (responds to partner's questions/comments).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to express appreciation for nature or make a polite request.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick oral or written (if capable) exit ticket by answering:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you can see in nature that makes you happy?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like talking about nature today? Why or why not?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about nature did you use today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite part about describing nature with your friend?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., pair activities, clear modeling of "can/can't").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to support students' speaking fluency and accuracy? (e.g., more sentence frames, flashcards for specific vocabulary, re-teaching pronunciation of /s/).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The speaking practice from today will prepare students to write about their favorite nature experiences in Lesson 4 (Writing Composition). They will describe their "My Favorite Nature Spot" in simple sentences and short paragraphs, building on the vocabulary and phrases used today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>